        "subtitle": "Behind the image",
        "text": "We all know what a fast-food restaurant looks like and what it sells. This is because when a big modern business sells fast food, it knows that we, its customers, are likely to want No Surprises. We are hungry and tired, and not in the mood for a celebration.\n\nThe building itself is easy to recognise, and it is designed to be a 'home from home', whether it is on the motorway or in the city. However, the things you usually find in a restaurant are a little different: the tables and chairs are bolted to the floor, and there is either no cutlery at all or none worth stealing. What is said and done is officially laid down, learned by the staff from handbooks and training sessions, and then picked up by customers in the course of regular visits. Things must be called by their correct names (cheeseburger, large fries), and the staff have a script they must learn – you know the kind of thing: 'Will that be with cheese, sir?', 'Have a nice day' – something I find particularly irritating. The staff wear a distinctive uniform, the menus are always the same and are even placed in the same spot in every outlet of the chain, prices are low, and the theme of cleanliness is repeated endlessly.\n\nThe company tries to play the role of a kind and caring parent. It knows that people are suspicious of large corporations, so it tries to stress its love for children. It often has cottage-style buildings, for warmth and cosiness, or large glass walls and smooth surfaces to show cleanliness and modernity. It responds to social concerns, but only if the beliefs are held widely enough to be 'correct'. Take, for example, the worry about fat in our diets. Fast-food chains only responded by putting salad on the menu when doctors and health ministers in various countries put pressure on them. At present, some chains are also busy showing how much they care about the environment.\n\nFast-food chains know that they are ordinary. They want to be ordinary, and to be thought of as almost inseparable from the idea of everyday food eaten outside the home. They are happy to let their customers have time off for holidays – Thanksgiving, Christmas and so on – which they do not cater for. Even these fairly rare holidays are turned into a profit, because the companies know that their favourite customers, families, are at home together then, watching television, where carefully placed advertisements will spread the news about new fast-food products and print the image of the various chains on their minds again for later. Families are the customers the fast-food chains want: good, law-abiding citizens who love their children and teach them how good hamburgers are for them. The chains even have very bright lighting to make sure that 'undesirable' people do not want to come in.\n\nSupplying a hamburger that is perfect in shape, weight and temperature, together with a choice from a fixed list of extras, to a customer who knows what to expect is a difficult thing to do. The technology involved has meant spending huge sums on research, and there are also political and economic questions to consider, such as how to keep up supplies of cheap beef and cheap buns. However, the image that is kept up is of a 'casual' eating experience. Make up your own mind, but there is more to a hamburger than just beef in a bread roll."
